(function(){'use strict';const{register,table,code,diagram}=window.AcademyContent;
register({id:61,centralQuestion:'When text asks the assistant to override its rules, what gives that text authority?',objective:'Distinguish direct and indirect prompt injection, instruction hierarchy and prompt leakage; enforce boundaries beyond natural-language instructions.',sections:[
{id:'authority',title:'Language can describe instructions without authority',body:['A language model processes tokens from many sources. The application must distinguish governing instructions, legitimate user intent and untrusted documents, pages or tool results. Prompt injection occurs when lower-trust content attempts to redirect behavior beyond its authority. Direct injection arrives through a user-facing prompt; indirect injection arrives through material read while doing another task.','Instruction hierarchy orders competing directions, but model compliance is probabilistic. A document claiming to be a system message does not become one. Provenance and application-assigned roles determine authority, not typography, urgency or claims of administrative status.','The central failure is confused authority: application-granted powers are exercised in response to attacker-controlled text. The objective may be a wrong answer, hidden-prompt disclosure, unauthorized retrieval or a tool action. The same sentence can be harmless quoted evidence in one task and a redirection attempt in another.'],table:table('Authority boundaries',['Input','Legitimate role','Must not gain'],[['Application policy','Defines behavior','Bypass of external controls'],['User request','Defines authorized task','Other users’ entitlements'],['Retrieved text','Evidence','System instruction authority'],['Tool output','Operation result','Permission for arbitrary next actions'],['Model proposal','Candidate action','Automatic approval']]),check:{question:'A PDF labels a paragraph SYSTEM OVERRIDE. What authority does it have?',answer:'Only document-content authority; the label cannot promote external text into application policy.'}},
{id:'controls',title:'Limit the consequences of manipulation',body:['Separate instructions and evidence in the prompt, preserve source labels and delimit quotations. These measures help but are not guarantees. Enforce authorization in ordinary code before sensitive retrieval or execution. A model cannot grant permission by producing a JSON field.','Allowlist tools and constrain arguments, destinations, spend and execution depth. Read-only tasks should receive read-only identities. Approval for a consequential action must bind to its exact parameters; changing recipient, amount or attachments invalidates earlier approval. Schema validity and authorization are independent checks.','Prompt leakage exposes hidden instructions or operational details. Keep secrets out of prompts; confidentiality of a hidden prompt must not be the primary protection for credentials. A scoped executor can use a secret store without showing the key to the model.'],diagram:diagram('flow','External authority boundary',['Untrusted text','Model proposal','Schema + policy check','Scoped executor','Audited result'],[[0,1],[1,2],[2,3],[3,4]],'The model proposes; caller identity and application policy authorize.'),failure:'Placing a key after “never reveal this secret” still exposes it to context and telemetry. Move credentials into the executor’s secret store.',check:{question:'Why does valid JSON not authorize a tool call?',answer:'Its target, action and parameters may exceed the caller’s permissions despite correct structure.'}},
{id:'evaluate',title:'Test effects rather than suspicious vocabulary',body:['Keyword filters miss paraphrases, multilingual or encoded attacks and can block legitimate security discussion. Measure unauthorized effects and data disclosure under a defined threat model, alongside benign-task completion and false refusals.','Use isolated tests with synthetic secrets and non-destructive tools: direct overrides, role spoofing, malicious tool outputs and conflicting documents. Evaluate multi-turn state as well as one-turn prompts. Preserve safe traces identifying where policy was lost.','When a failure occurs, contain the capability and repair the missing boundary. Stronger wording may reduce recurrence, but narrow permissions, validated destinations and explicit approval usually provide more dependable limits on harm.'],example:'A retrieved page asks the summarizer to send private context to an outside address. It remains evidence; the executor rejects any send proposal because the task has no outbound-send authorization.',check:{question:'What is stronger than counting blocked attack phrases?',answer:'Measuring actual unauthorized actions/disclosures and benign false positives by capability and severity.'}}
],glossary:[['Direct prompt injection','Redirection through the direct prompt channel.'],['Indirect prompt injection','Redirection embedded in external task evidence.'],['Instruction hierarchy','Ordering of instruction authority.'],['Prompt leakage','Exposure of hidden prompt or operational instructions.'],['Confused deputy','Privileged component acting on less-privileged directions.']],exercise:{duration:40,title:'Secure a document summarizer',brief:'A summarizer reads internal files and can propose sending summaries; some files contain hostile directions.',parts:['Assign trust and authority to every input.','Separate read and send permissions.','Bind approval to recipient and content.','Create four hostile and four benign tests.'],solution:'Retrieve only caller-authorized files and treat their text as evidence. Give summarization no send capability. If sending is explicitly requested, validate destination, content and exact approval outside generation. Test role spoofing, external destinations and quoted benign security text; score unauthorized effects and legitimate completion with synthetic data.'},sources:{core:['owaspPrompt','owaspLLM'],deep:['nistGenAI']},quiz:[
{concept:'Authority',prompt:'A retrieved memo claims to be a new system instruction. How should it be handled?',answer:0,options:[['As document content.','Its source does not gain authority from its claim.'],['Above application policy.','That improperly promotes external text.'],['As administrator authentication.','Text is not identity proof.'],['As permission to run tools.','Tool authority comes from policy.']]},
{concept:'Direct injection',prompt:'A user asks a bot to ignore policy and disclose hidden operational instructions. Which channel is attacked?',answer:1,options:[['Training data only.','No training change is described.'],['Direct prompt injection.','The redirection is in the user prompt.'],['GPU scheduling.','No hardware issue exists.'],['Guaranteed extraction of all weights.','That outcome is not established.']]},
{concept:'Indirect injection',prompt:'A search result tells an assistant to send private context outside the organization. What makes it indirect?',answer:2,options:[['Its language.','Language does not define the channel.'],['Parameter count.','Size is irrelevant.'],['Delivery through retrieved material.','Task evidence carries the instruction.'],['It is harmless.','The requested effect is dangerous.']]},
{concept:'Tool authorization',prompt:'Valid JSON proposes deleting another tenant’s file. What must stop it?',answer:3,options:[['JSON parsing alone.','The structure is valid.'],['Temperature zero.','Determinism is not authorization.'],['A polite prompt alone.','It is not an enforceable boundary.'],['Caller-scoped action authorization.','The resource is outside entitlement.']]},
{concept:'Prompt secrets',prompt:'A production key is embedded in a hidden prompt. What is the sound repair?',answer:0,options:[['Use a secret store in a scoped executor.','The model need not see the key.'],['Repeat nondisclosure wording.','The secret remains in context.'],['Log the key for debugging.','That expands exposure.'],['Publish the prompt.','That leaks the credential.']]},
{concept:'Approval binding',prompt:'After approval, an agent changes email recipient and attachments. Does approval still apply?',answer:1,options:[['Yes, any send is covered.','Approval was specific.'],['No; bind approval to exact action details.','Changed effects need new authority.'],['Yes if JSON validates.','Structure is not consent.'],['Yes if confidence is high.','Confidence grants no permission.']]},
{concept:'Security evaluation',prompt:'A filter blocks “ignore” but misses paraphrased tool abuse. What should testing measure?',answer:2,options:[['Only keyword count.','It is easy to game.'],['Only answer length.','It does not measure security.'],['Unauthorized effects/disclosures and benign false positives.','These assess the boundary.'],['Training accuracy only.','It is not the attack surface.']]}
]});
register({id:62,centralQuestion:'Did the attacker bypass a behavior policy, expose private data, or exploit both?',objective:'Distinguish jailbreaks, injection and disclosure; protect sensitive information across training, retrieval, context, outputs and logs.',sections:[
{id:'bypass',title:'Policy bypass and authority confusion can overlap',body:['A jailbreak attempts to bypass behavioral safeguards through reframing, role play, obfuscation or adversarial conversation. Prompt injection concerns redirection across instruction trust boundaries. A direct attack may be both. Classify mechanism, objective and consequence instead of assuming every label is mutually exclusive.','Multi-turn attacks can accumulate apparently harmless steps into an unsafe outcome. A defense tested only on obvious single-turn requests may miss this. Evaluate the whole task and tool effects, including false refusals on legitimate security discussion.','A refusal is one layer. Retrieval authorization, output validation and tool permissions remain necessary when generation behaves unexpectedly. Limiting available authority reduces the maximum consequence of a bypass.'],table:table('Distinct security questions',['Category','Question'],[['Jailbreak','Can behavioral safeguards be bypassed?'],['Injection','Can lower-trust text redirect authority?'],['Disclosure','Can protected information leave its boundary?'],['Prompt leakage','Can hidden operational instructions be exposed?']]),check:{question:'Can one attack be both jailbreak and injection?',answer:'Yes: it can redirect instruction handling while seeking behavior outside safeguards.'}},
{id:'privacy',title:'The final answer is only one disclosure channel',body:['PII identifies or can be linked to a person in context. Credentials, commercial secrets and private records are also sensitive. Inventory copies in ingestion, training, features, retrieval, model context, tools, telemetry and exports. Redacting a final answer does not protect raw prompts stored in broad-access logs.','Training exposure can reproduce memorized content; retrieval leakage brings unauthorized documents into context; logging exposure distributes sensitive requests to diagnostic systems. Controls differ: minimize and govern training data, authorize before retrieval, and restrict/redact/expire telemetry.','Removing names does not guarantee anonymity. Quasi-identifiers such as exact dates, location and rare events can enable linkage. Hashing a predictable identifier is not automatically anonymization. State the risk reduction and residual linkage possibilities explicitly.'],failure:'Redacted responses with unredacted, indefinitely retained traces move the disclosure into another channel.',check:{question:'Why can nameless records still identify someone?',answer:'Combinations of quasi-identifiers can be linked with external information.'}},
{id:'response',title:'Protect use as well as storage',body:['Minimize data sent to models, scope access to purpose and define retention. Encrypt traffic and stored copies, but remember that an authorized overprivileged application can still disclose decrypted data. Keep secrets out of prompts and ordinary logs.','Pattern filters help detect common PII formats but miss indirect references and unusual structures. Review false positives and negatives by data type and context. Escalate ambiguous high-impact output rather than equating a clean regex result with safety.','An exposure incident requires containment, credential revocation where applicable, restricted evidence, affected-record analysis and the organization’s notification process. Deleting logs without investigation may destroy the evidence needed to understand scope. Validate repairs across every data channel.'],example:'A bot retrieves another department’s personnel file from a global index. Phone-number redaction may catch one field but miss performance details. The primary repair is authorized retrieval, with output filtering as another layer.',check:{question:'Can encryption at rest stop an overprivileged application from returning records?',answer:'No. Access and use policy must constrain the application after decryption.'}}
],glossary:[['Jailbreak','Attempt to bypass behavioral safeguards.'],['PII','Information identifying or linkable to a person.'],['Quasi-identifier','Attribute that helps identify a person in combination.'],['Data minimization','Limiting data use to what a purpose requires.'],['Logging exposure','Sensitive information accessible through telemetry.']],exercise:{duration:40,title:'Investigate a trace-system privacy leak',brief:'A bot refuses a private phone-number request, but the retrieved HR file appears in developer-accessible traces.',parts:['Classify successful and failed controls.','Map data copies and readers.','Design authorization, redaction and retention.','Specify containment and adversarial/benign tests.'],solution:'Output refusal succeeded but retrieval/telemetry protection failed. Scope retrieval, minimize context, restrict/redact traces and apply retention. Preserve controlled evidence, assess access and repair permissions. Test disclosure in responses, logs and tool arguments; measure false positives as well as missed private fields.'},sources:{core:['owaspLLM','nistGenAI'],deep:['nistAIRMF']},quiz:[
{concept:'Jailbreak',prompt:'An adversarial conversation induces prohibited output without modifying training data. What objective fits?',answer:1,options:[['Necessarily poisoning.','Training is unchanged.'],['Behavioral-safeguard bypass.','This is the jailbreak objective.'],['Hardware extraction.','No hardware access exists.'],['Proof of safety.','The bypass indicates risk.']]},
{concept:'Logging exposure',prompt:'The answer is redacted but raw private records remain in broad-access traces. Has disclosure been prevented?',answer:0,options:[['No; telemetry is another channel.','Protection spans the data lifecycle.'],['Yes; only final text matters.','Logs have readers.'],['Yes if JSON is used.','Format is not access control.'],['All monitoring must cease.','Minimized controlled telemetry is possible.']]},
{concept:'Reidentification',prompt:'Exact birth date, small town and rare diagnosis remain after removing names. What is the risk?',answer:2,options:[['No linkage is possible.','The combination may identify someone.'],['Only slower inference.','The issue is privacy.'],['Reidentification through quasi-identifiers.','External records can provide linkage.'],['Automatic public-data status.','Transformation is not publication consent.']]},
{concept:'Encryption limits',prompt:'An overprivileged service returns decrypted HR records. Would disk encryption alone stop it?',answer:3,options:[['Yes, applications cannot decrypt.','Authorized services can.'],['Yes with longer keys.','Key length does not fix permissions.'],['Yes with HTTPS.','Transport is separate.'],['No; constrain access and use.','Encryption addresses other threats.']]},
{concept:'Retrieval leakage',prompt:'Global search exposes another tenant’s file before redaction. What primary control is missing?',answer:0,options:[['Authorization-scoped retrieval.','Private evidence should not enter context.'],['Shorter answers.','Short answers can leak.'],['Higher temperature.','It is not access control.'],['More citations.','Attribution does not authorize access.']]},
{concept:'Credential incident',prompt:'A live key appears in traces. What should happen immediately?',answer:1,options:[['Hide only the dashboard row.','Copies may already exist.'],['Revoke/rotate, contain and investigate access.','The exposed authority must be removed.'],['Reuse it elsewhere.','That spreads risk.'],['Ask the model to forget.','That does not revoke credentials.']]},
{concept:'Filter quality',prompt:'A filter blocks invoice IDs but misses unusual phone formats. What is needed?',answer:2,options:[['Only block counts.','They hide errors.'],['Only fluency scoring.','Unrelated.'],['Reviewed false positives/negatives by context and type.','This locates detection failures.'],['Only larger model size.','Capacity is not evaluation.']]}
]});
register({id:63,centralQuestion:'Can a relevant document remain untrusted to command the assistant?',objective:'Secure RAG ingestion, retrieval and context assembly with provenance, ACLs, source validation and injection-aware tool controls.',sections:[
{id:'ingest',title:'The corpus is a data supply chain',body:['A corpus has publishers, parsers, ingestion jobs and revision history. Attackers may insert documents, alter a legitimate source or hide instructions in fields a casual reviewer overlooks. Validate sources and formats, preserve provenance, scan content and record immutable versions.','An approved repository may contain user-controlled comments. Trust therefore follows the actual content origin, sometimes at field or section level. Hosting domain alone does not establish authority or factual quality.','Parse without executing embedded content. Retain document ID, revision, access labels, timestamps and chunk lineage. Dropping parent ACLs during chunking can expose private material; dropping revision identity makes citations and rollback unreliable.'],check:{question:'Why is an internal wiki not uniformly trusted?',answer:'It may contain comments or uploads from lower-trust authors; authority depends on the specific content source.'}},
{id:'retrieve',title:'Relevance does not establish permission or truth',body:['Vector similarity estimates relevance in an embedding space. It proves neither truth, freshness, authorization nor instruction priority. Apply caller and document entitlement before evidence reaches generation. Post-generation redaction cannot undo the model’s exposure to private context.','Derive tenant scope from authenticated server-side identity. Model-generated filter arguments must not override entitlement. Preserve ACLs and source versions through hybrid search, reranking and context construction.','Label retrieved content as evidence and keep it separate from governing instructions. Require supported claims, citations and abstention when evidence is inadequate. These prompting measures reduce confusion while external controls enforce actual access and action boundaries.'],diagram:diagram('rag','Secure evidence path',['Authorized task','ACL-filtered retrieval','Provenance/content checks','Evidence-only context','Validated answer/action'],[[0,1],[1,2],[2,3],[3,4]],'A retrieved chunk never acquires authority to change policy or tool scope.'),failure:'Accepting an arbitrary tenant ID from the model turns a retrieval parameter into an access-control bypass.',check:{question:'What does high cosine similarity fail to prove?',answer:'Permission, truth, freshness, integrity and instruction authority.'}},
{id:'attack',title:'Follow the attack from evidence to consequence',body:['A malicious chunk may first provide useful information, then request a new task or destination. The failure occurs when evidence becomes instruction and a privileged tool acts on it. Constrain tools, recipients, arguments and budgets outside generation; require exact-action approval where appropriate.','Content filtering can quarantine known patterns but may miss subtle attacks or block legitimate security manuals. Use source controls, capability limits and outcome-based tests together. Read-only summarization has a smaller consequence surface than an agent with email and database writes.','Evaluate retrieval usefulness and security jointly. Test synthetic hostile documents, benign quoted instructions, conflicting policies, outdated sources and cross-tenant probes. Measure unauthorized retrieval, unsupported claims, citation errors, tool violations and task completion. Better recall can increase exposure to malicious material if boundaries are weak.'],example:'A travel-policy chunk instructs the agent to email receipts to a new address. The assistant may use travel facts but must not treat the document as authorization to send; the executor rejects the unapproved destination.',check:{question:'Why can improved retrieval recall increase risk?',answer:'More relevant attacker-controlled content may enter context unless source, access and authority controls remain intact.'}}
],glossary:[['Provenance','Origin, author, revision and ingestion history.'],['Chunk lineage','Mapping from a segment to its parent source/version.'],['ACL filtering','Retrieval restricted by caller access rights.'],['Source validation','Checking origin, integrity and suitability of evidence.'],['Evidence-only context','Content treated as data rather than governing instructions.']],exercise:{duration:40,title:'Harden a policy index',brief:'Employees upload notes into a shared RAG index; the assistant can draft emails.',parts:['Define source and chunk metadata.','Enforce server-derived access filters.','Separate evidence and tool authority.','Create eight security/usefulness tests.'],solution:'Retain uploader/source/revision/ACL lineage and validate files without executing content. Derive access from identity and preserve it through ranking/citations. Treat chunks as evidence; sending is a separate approved action. Test spoofed roles, external destinations, private documents, outdated policy and benign security quotations.'},sources:{core:['owaspPrompt','ragPaper'],deep:['nistGenAI']},quiz:[
{concept:'Relevance and trust',prompt:'A malicious chunk ranks first at cosine 0.94. What follows?',answer:0,options:[['It may be relevant but has no instruction authority.','Similarity does not establish trust.'],['It overrides policy.','Ranking is not authority.'],['It must be true.','Similarity is not truth.'],['It grants all-tenant access.','Permissions are separate.']]},
{concept:'ACL enforcement',prompt:'The model proposes another tenant_id in a search tool call. What should the server do?',answer:1,options:[['Trust valid JSON.','Structure does not prove entitlement.'],['Validate against authenticated server-side scope.','Identity determines permitted resources.'],['Remove filters.','That broadens exposure.'],['Ask the document for permission.','Documents cannot authorize.']]},
{concept:'Chunk lineage',prompt:'Chunking removes parent access labels and revision IDs. What is harmed?',answer:2,options:[['Only typography.','The issue is evidence/security.'],['Nothing if vectors exist.','Vectors do not preserve ACL semantics.'],['Authorization, citation verification and change tracing.','Parent metadata is necessary.'],['Only training speed.','This affects retrieval.']]},
{concept:'Tool boundary',prompt:'A retrieved policy orders email to an external address. What should reject an unapproved send?',answer:3,options:[['Cosine similarity.','It measures relevance.'],['Model confidence.','It is not permission.'],['Word count.','Short leaks still harm.'],['External action policy and destination/content authorization.','The document cannot authorize the action.']]},
{concept:'Source trust',prompt:'Anonymous comments sit on an approved wiki. Should they inherit administrator authority?',answer:0,options:[['No; assess actual origin.','Hosting is not author authority.'],['Yes, all text on the domain is policy.','That confuses location and trust.'],['Yes if recent.','Freshness is not privilege.'],['Yes if formatted officially.','Formatting is spoofable.']]},
{concept:'Filter trade-off',prompt:'A defense removes every security manual mentioning injection. What is missing?',answer:1,options:[['More block counts.','That rewards overblocking.'],['Benign controls and coverage/false-positive evaluation.','Legitimate retrieval is damaged.'],['Higher temperature.','It cannot restore evidence safely.'],['Removal of all security.','Layered controls remain needed.']]},
{concept:'Context exposure',prompt:'Unauthorized records enter context but final output is filtered. Why is this flawed?',answer:2,options:[['Filters never help.','They can be another layer.'],['Models cannot use context.','They generate from it.'],['Private information already entered the model path.','Authorize before retrieval.'],['Citations authorize access.','Attribution is not entitlement.']]}
]});
register({id:64,centralQuestion:'Is the attacker changing training, probing membership, copying behavior, or perturbing one prediction?',objective:'Classify extraction, membership inference, adversarial examples, poisoning and backdoors by stage, access and defensive evidence.',sections:[
{id:'mechanisms',title:'Stage and objective identify the mechanism',body:['An adversarial example modifies an inference input to induce an undesirable prediction under relevant constraints. Data poisoning changes training/adaptation data; model poisoning alters updates or artifacts. A backdoor creates trigger-conditioned behavior while ordinary performance may remain normal.','Model extraction seeks to copy model behavior or assets through queries or artifact access. Membership inference estimates whether a record participated in training. Extraction concerns capability or intellectual property; membership concerns privacy. Neither automatically implies the other.','State attacker knowledge and access: labels, scores, gradients, data writes, updates or artifacts. Authentication can limit query abuse but does not repair poison already accepted into training. A mitigation must address the actual capability.'],table:table('Threat classification',['Attack','Stage/goal','Control evidence'],[['Adversarial example','Inference misbehavior','Threat-specific robustness tests'],['Poisoning','Training/adaptation manipulation','Provenance, access and data review'],['Backdoor','Triggered hidden behavior','Targeted trigger/slice tests'],['Extraction','Copy behavior/assets','Query monitoring and artifact protection'],['Membership inference','Infer training inclusion','Privacy evaluation and minimization']]),check:{question:'Can normal accuracy rule out a backdoor?',answer:'No; a rare trigger can activate hidden behavior while ordinary examples remain accurate.'}},
{id:'supply',title:'Training feedback and model files are security boundaries',body:['Track source provenance and access for datasets, labels, user feedback and external weights. Unreviewed feedback is not automatically ground truth; coordinated submissions can steer continuous training. Separate collection from approval and detect anomalous source influence.','Verify artifact origin and integrity. Some model serialization formats execute code during loading; a familiar extension and high benchmark score do not establish safety. Use trusted registries, controlled loaders, least privilege and isolated evaluation.','Test targeted slices and plausible triggers in addition to averages. Compare behavior across data and artifact revisions. Robustness to image perturbations does not establish resistance to document injection or extraction; claims must remain specific to evaluated threats.'],failure:'Automatically learning from every user correction lets an attacker control labels without repository access.',check:{question:'Why evaluate a third-party model in isolation before production loading?',answer:'The artifact may execute code or contain hidden behavior despite strong average benchmarks.'}},
{id:'privacy',title:'Privacy guarantees need defined mechanisms',body:['Overfitting and memorization can increase exposure, but ordinary generalization metrics do not prove privacy. Evaluate membership attacks against matched members and nonmembers, reporting false positives. Full confidence vectors may expose more information than bounded decisions, though limiting outputs affects legitimate uses.','Differential privacy bounds how much an individual’s data affects an algorithm’s output under a specified mechanism. It requires a neighboring-data definition, calibrated randomness, privacy budget and composition accounting. Arbitrary noise is not a formal guarantee, and stronger privacy can reduce utility.','Combine minimization, retention, controlled outputs, access limits and monitoring. Rate limits raise extraction cost but do not prove impossibility. Investigate suspicious queries with restricted evidence and contain affected capabilities while evaluating exposure.'],example:'A classifier is more confident on exact training records. This is a privacy signal, not proof that every confident response reveals membership; compare attack performance against realistic nonmembers.',check:{question:'Why is adding random noise not enough to claim differential privacy?',answer:'The guarantee depends on a defined mechanism, calibrated noise, budget and composition, not noise alone.'}}
],glossary:[['Adversarial example','Crafted inference input causing undesirable prediction.'],['Data poisoning','Malicious change to training/adaptation data.'],['Backdoor','Trigger-conditioned hidden model behavior.'],['Model extraction','Attempt to copy behavior or model assets.'],['Membership inference','Inference of a record’s training inclusion.'],['Differential privacy','Formal bound on individual-data influence under a specified algorithm.']],exercise:{duration:40,title:'Triage model security events',brief:'A sticker breaks vision predictions, a feedback campaign changes labels, a phrase triggers odd behavior and millions of systematic API probes arrive.',parts:['Classify mechanism, stage and access.','Identify distinguishing evidence.','Choose containment and prevention.','Explain limits of accuracy and rate-limit evidence.'],solution:'Investigate inference perturbation, data poisoning, backdoor behavior and extraction/privacy probing respectively. Preserve source/artifact/query lineage, isolate suspicious updates, test triggers and restrict query authority. Normal accuracy misses targeted behavior; rate limits constrain cost without proving attacks impossible.'},sources:{core:['nistAML','owaspLLM'],deep:['nistGenAI']},quiz:[
{concept:'Poisoning',prompt:'Misleading user labels enter retraining. Which boundary was attacked?',answer:0,options:[['Training feedback/data.','The learned signal is manipulated.'],['Only routing.','The change is in labels.'],['Only GPU scheduling.','No resource attack is described.'],['Only formatting.','Labels influence learning.']]},
{concept:'Backdoor',prompt:'A rare visual mark triggers misclassification while normal accuracy remains high. What deserves investigation?',answer:1,options:[['Only latency.','Behavior is trigger-dependent.'],['A possible backdoor.','Ordinary accuracy can hide it.'],['Guaranteed label shift.','No prevalence evidence exists.'],['No issue.','Targeted harm remains.']]},
{concept:'Membership',prompt:'An attacker wants to know if one patient was in training. Which objective is this?',answer:2,options:[['Compression.','No compression goal exists.'],['Autoscaling.','Unrelated.'],['Membership inference.','The question concerns inclusion.'],['Calibration only.','Calibration concerns probability reliability.']]},
{concept:'Extraction',prompt:'Systematic API queries train a substitute decision model. Which objective fits?',answer:3,options:[['Imputation.','No missing-data task exists.'],['Only a trigger attack.','The goal is copying.'],['Necessarily an authorized audit.','Authorization is not established.'],['Model extraction.','Queries imitate behavior.']]},
{concept:'Artifact safety',prompt:'Unknown executable model serialization scores well. What is required?',answer:0,options:[['Trusted provenance, controlled loading and isolation.','Quality does not establish safety.'],['Load with production credentials.','That exposes authority.'],['Ignore the format.','Loading may execute code.'],['Rename the extension.','Names do not change behavior.']]},
{concept:'Differential privacy',prompt:'A team adds arbitrary noise and claims formal privacy. What is missing?',answer:1,options:[['A marketing label.','The issue is a formal mechanism.'],['Adjacency, calibrated mechanism, budget and composition.','These define the guarantee.'],['A larger font.','Irrelevant.'],['A promise not to test utility.','Utility still matters.']]},
{concept:'Rate limits',prompt:'Does API rate limiting prove extraction impossible?',answer:2,options:[['Yes, any limit prevents learning.','Queries can accumulate.'],['Yes if JSON is returned.','Format still conveys information.'],['No; it raises cost and needs other controls.','It is one defense layer.'],['Access control is unnecessary.','It remains useful.']]}
]});
register({id:65,centralQuestion:'The caller proved an identity—but which records and actions may that identity use now?',objective:'Design authentication, authorization, IAM/RBAC, least privilege, secret rotation and encryption boundaries for AI services.',sections:[
{id:'identity',title:'Identity and permission answer different questions',body:['Authentication verifies identity. Authorization decides whether that identity may perform an action on a resource under current conditions. A valid token does not authorize every tenant or endpoint. Validate signature, issuer, audience, expiry and relevant claims before relying on the identity.','IAM manages identities, roles, policies and credentials. RBAC grants permissions through roles; conditions can further restrict tenant, environment or ownership. Least privilege limits authority to the task and duration. Separate training, serving, promotion and tool-execution identities.','Enforce policy at data and action boundaries, not only the interface. Hiding a button does not stop direct API requests. Vector retrieval, registry promotion and outbound tools each need a policy decision using server-side resource metadata.'],table:table('Security control',['Question','Failure'],[['Authentication','Who calls?','Forged/expired token accepted'],['Authorization','May this action target this resource?','Cross-tenant access'],['RBAC','What does the role permit?','Serving identity promotes models'],['Encryption','Can observers read bytes?','Plaintext transport/storage'],['Audit','Can action history be reconstructed?','No actor/resource record']]),check:{question:'Why should serving normally lack promotion permission?',answer:'It needs approved artifacts, not authority to change the production release.'}},
{id:'secrets',title:'Credentials need a complete operational lifecycle',body:['Use a secret manager or managed workload identity instead of code, images, notebooks or prompts. Inject credentials only into the executor that needs them. Do not print environment variables or full headers; log references and identities instead of values.','Rotation creates a replacement, updates consumers, verifies use and revokes the old key. Temporary overlap may preserve availability, but indefinite overlap leaves old authority exposed. Processes may cache credentials until reload, so verify every consumer before revocation.','Prefer short-lived scoped credentials where available. Development access should not reach production data. The model must not choose a more privileged identity through tool arguments. Emergency revocation should work independently of the model-release schedule.'],failure:'Updating the secret store while replicas retain an old in-memory key can cause a fleet outage when the old key is revoked.',check:{question:'What completes rotation?',answer:'Verified consumer cutover, revocation of old credentials and an audit record—not merely creating a new key.'}},
{id:'protect',title:'Encryption protects bytes; policy controls their use',body:['Encryption in transit protects communication when endpoints and certificates are verified. Encryption at rest protects stored copies according to key management. Neither stops a legitimately decrypting but overprivileged application from disclosing data.','Keys need ownership, access policy, rotation and recovery. Include backups, telemetry vendors and model providers in the data-flow inventory; every copy needs access and retention rules.','Audit actor, operation, resource, policy result, release and correlation ID without recording secrets. If authorization cannot be established for a protected action, fail safely or escalate; bypassing policy during an identity outage converts an availability issue into a security incident.'],code:code('python','principal = verify_token(token, audience="model-api")\nresource = documents.get_metadata(document_id)\nif not policy.allows(principal, "read", resource):\n    raise PermissionError("Not authorized")\nreturn documents.read(document_id)',['Audience validation establishes identity for this service.','Policy uses server-side resource metadata.','The sensitive read happens only after authorization.'],'A valid token cannot become permission for arbitrary document IDs.'),check:{question:'What if authorization cannot be checked for a sensitive write?',answer:'Do not execute; defer or use the defined safe escalation path.'}}
],glossary:[['Authentication','Verification of identity.'],['Authorization','Permission decision for action and resource.'],['IAM','Identity, credential and policy management.'],['RBAC','Role-based permission assignment.'],['Least privilege','Minimum necessary task authority.'],['Secret rotation','Replacement, consumer cutover and revocation.'],['Encryption in transit','Protection of communicating data.'],['Encryption at rest','Protection of stored data.']],exercise:{duration:35,title:'Replace a shared all-powerful key',brief:'One key lets training, inference and an agent read every file, promote models and send email.',parts:['Split roles and identities.','Design resource-scoped checks.','Write staged rotation and emergency revocation.','Specify encryption and safe audit fields.'],solution:'Training reads approved snapshots and writes candidates; inference reads approved artifacts; promotion has separate approval; retrieval follows caller ACLs; sending uses a scoped approved executor. Stage replacement credentials, verify refresh, revoke old authority. Audit actor/action/resource/policy/release without keys and protect all stored/transmitted copies.'},sources:{core:['owaspLLM','kubernetesBasics'],deep:['nistAIRMF']},quiz:[
{concept:'Authorization',prompt:'A valid employee token requests a restricted departmental file. What decides access?',answer:0,options:[['Current resource-specific entitlement.','Identity alone is insufficient.'],['Token validity alone.','That only proves identity.'],['Model confidence.','Not permission.'],['Button visibility.','APIs can be called directly.']]},
{concept:'Least privilege',prompt:'An inference identity can promote registry models. Why is this excessive?',answer:1,options:[['Serving needs every control-plane power.','It normally loads approved artifacts.'],['Compromise could change production releases.','Separate promotion authority.'],['Roles cannot be split.','They can.'],['Only image size matters.','This is authority risk.']]},
{concept:'Audience',prompt:'A token for another service is accepted by the model API. What validation is missing?',answer:2,options:[['Response length.','Unrelated.'],['Model version.','Not a token check.'],['Audience.','The token must target this service.'],['CPU limit.','Not identity validation.']]},
{concept:'Rotation',prompt:'A new key exists, but old keys remain valid indefinitely. What is incomplete?',answer:3,options:[['Nothing.','Old authority remains.'],['Only UI styling.','Credential lifecycle is incomplete.'],['Retraining.','Not required here.'],['Verified cutover and revocation.','Rotation must remove old authority.']]},
{concept:'Encryption',prompt:'HTTPS is enabled but a service reads all tenants. What remains?',answer:0,options:[['Overbroad authorization.','Transport protection does not narrow access.'],['No risk.','Unauthorized records can be returned.'],['Automatic least privilege.','HTTPS does not grant roles.'],['No audit need.','Actions still need traceability.']]},
{concept:'Fail safely',prompt:'The policy service is down during a sensitive write. What is appropriate?',answer:1,options:[['Permit all writes.','That bypasses authorization.'],['Deny/defer with safe escalation.','Authority is not established.'],['Ask the model to guess.','It is not the policy authority.'],['Use another tenant’s token.','That violates access boundaries.']]},
{concept:'Audit',prompt:'Which audit record is most useful and safe?',answer:2,options:[['Raw key/password.','Never log credentials.'],['Only “success”.','It omits responsibility and target.'],['Actor, action, resource, policy result, release and correlation ID.','It reconstructs the action without secrets.'],['Only token count.','It lacks authorization evidence.']]}
]});
register({id:66,centralQuestion:'Who can stop an unsafe decision, and what evidence makes that authority usable?',objective:'Combine guardrails, agent permissions, human oversight, fairness, explainability and governance into an auditable lifecycle.',sections:[
{id:'guardrails',title:'Controls belong at distinct failure boundaries',body:['Input filtering screens requests and evidence; output filtering checks generated content; schema validation constrains structure; policy checks enforce permitted behavior; tool restrictions limit effects. Valid JSON can still contain an unauthorized payment, and harmless-looking prose can expose private facts.','Agents expand the action surface. Restrict operations, destinations, arguments, spend and execution depth. Read-only tasks need read-only credentials. Human approval must bind to exact consequential parameters and provide enough evidence for a meaningful decision.','Oversight fails when reviewers lack time, expertise or authority and merely rubber-stamp outputs. Show uncertainty, sources, alternatives and consequences; provide override and stop controls. Measure workload and disagreement so human capacity is part of the design.'],diagram:diagram('flow','Governed action',['Proposal + evidence','Policy + scope','Meaningful approval','Scoped executor','Audit + monitor + stop'],[[0,1],[1,2],[2,3],[3,4]],'Independent controls limit effects even when generation is manipulated or mistaken.'),check:{question:'What makes approval meaningful?',answer:'A competent reviewer sees the exact action/evidence, has time and authority to refuse, and changed parameters invalidate approval.'}},
{id:'responsible',title:'Fairness and explanation depend on the decision',body:['Bias can enter sampling, labels, features, objectives, thresholds and feedback. Evaluate meaningful subgroups and intersections with sample sizes and uncertainty. Equal aggregate accuracy does not imply equal false-negative burden. Fairness criteria can conflict under differing base rates; select criteria from harm and context with accountable stakeholders.','Global explanations summarize behavior; local explanations address an individual case. Feature attribution is not automatically causal: correlations and reference choices affect it. A low attribution for a protected feature does not rule out proxy effects or disparate errors.','Transparency communicates intended use, limitations, automation and recourse. Accountability assigns owners for effects. Privacy and fairness can interact when subgroup evaluation requires sensitive attributes; justify access, protect data and document the trade-off.'],table:table('Responsible-AI evidence',['Concern','Evidence/action'],[['Fairness','Subgroup errors/calibration with uncertainty'],['Explainability','Faithful global/local explanations'],['Transparency','Intended use, limits and user recourse'],['Oversight','Review capacity, override and stop tests'],['Privacy','Data-flow/access/retention controls'],['Safety','Hazard severity and containment tests']]),failure:'A feature-attribution chart with low age importance does not prove age-related fairness; correlated proxies and decision thresholds still matter.',check:{question:'Why is attribution not causality?',answer:'It explains model behavior relative to a method/reference; intervention effects require additional causal assumptions and evidence.'}},
{id:'governance',title:'Risk must connect to owners and deployed evidence',body:['Classify risk by intended use, affected people, severity, likelihood, scale, reversibility and uncertainty. Creative drafting and clinical triage need different gates. NIST AI RMF’s Govern, Map, Measure and Manage functions organize responsibility, context, evidence and response; using them is not a claim of certification compliance.','A model or system card records intended/excluded uses, data/evaluation scope, slices, limitations, ethical considerations and owners. Link it to actual model, prompt, data, code and release versions. Documentation detached from production is weak audit evidence.','Prompt and index changes can alter risk as much as weights. Apply proportionate evaluation, approval, canary, monitoring and rollback. Define retirement and ongoing review; governance does not finish at launch.'],check:{question:'Why should prompt updates pass change gates?',answer:'They can materially change refusals, outputs, tool actions and harm without changing weights.'}},
{id:'escalation',title:'Make escalation possible before harm becomes an outage',body:['Define severity, containment authority, technical response, business/risk communication and evidence retention. Ethical concerns may appear before technical failure: a subgroup is denied service or users lack recourse. Staff need a route to pause and raise concerns without first proving a complete root cause.','During an incident, stop the harmful capability, preserve restricted evidence, identify affected cases and coordinate remediation. After recovery, identify the failed control and verify corrections on protected cases. Record who accepted residual risk and why.','Readiness combines quality, fairness, security, reliability, oversight capacity and ownership. No single score substitutes for this evidence. The engineer makes unresolved risk visible to someone with authority to accept, reduce or reject it.'],example:'A screening model meets average recall but misses more cases in a small subgroup. Inspect uncertainty and label quality, assess severity, pause expansion if material, and design remediation or reviewed use rather than dismissing the group’s size.',check:{question:'What should an engineer do with a material pre-release ethical concern?',answer:'Document evidence and uncertainty and use the accountable pause/escalation process.'}}
],glossary:[['Guardrail','Control constraining input, output, policy or action.'],['Human oversight','Effective human understanding, intervention and accountability.'],['Subgroup evaluation','Measuring outcomes for meaningful population slices.'],['Model card','Documentation of use, evidence, limitations and responsibility.'],['Risk classification','Assessment selecting controls from severity, likelihood and exposure.'],['Auditability','Ability to reconstruct artifacts, actions and responsible actors.'],['Recourse','Meaningful path to challenge or improve an adverse decision.']],exercise:{duration:90,title:'Govern a screening assistant',brief:'The assistant prioritizes cases and drafts referrals. One subgroup has lower recall; staff can review only 30 minutes daily.',parts:['Classify risks and intended/excluded uses.','Design tool scope and exact-action approval.','Specify subgroup evidence, explanations and recourse.','Create a system-card/release evidence outline.','Define review capacity, incident escalation and rollback; take D6 exam.'],solution:'Keep clinical authority with qualified staff; restrict automation to prioritization/drafting and require referral approval. Measure sensitivity/specificity/calibration by subgroup with uncertainty and label review. Fit volume to real review capacity or reduce scope. Link documentation to release identities, define stop triggers and restricted evidence, and require accountable acceptance before expansion.'},sources:{core:['nistAIRMF','nistGenAI','owaspLLM'],deep:['googleFairness','microsoftRAI']},quiz:[
{concept:'Guardrail layers',prompt:'Valid JSON transfers funds to an unauthorized account. Which check is missing?',answer:0,options:[['Action authorization/policy.','Structure and permission differ.'],['JSON syntax only.','It is valid.'],['Tokenization only.','Tokens do not grant authority.'],['Only model registration.','The executor boundary is central.']]},
{concept:'Oversight',prompt:'Reviewers approve 500 complex cases in ten minutes without evidence. What is wrong?',answer:1,options:[['Any click guarantees safety.','Review may be meaningless.'],['Capacity and information do not support effective oversight.','Approval must be feasible and informed.'],['Humans should never review.','A sound workflow can help.'],['Only interface color matters.','Decision capability is the issue.']]},
{concept:'Fairness',prompt:'Aggregate accuracy is stable but a subgroup’s false negatives double. What should be assessed?',answer:2,options:[['Only the aggregate.','It hides burden.'],['Only feature count.','Count is not harm.'],['Subgroup harm, uncertainty, labels and mitigation.','Consequences differ despite averages.'],['Nothing if the group is small.','Severity can outweigh volume.']]},
{concept:'Explainability',prompt:'Income attribution is high. Does this prove an income intervention causes the shown outcome change?',answer:3,options:[['Yes, all attribution is causal.','It generally explains model behavior.'],['Yes, correlation proves intervention.','It does not.'],['Yes, precise charts prove causality.','Precision is not identification.'],['No; causal evidence and assumptions are needed.','References and correlations affect attribution.']]},
{concept:'Change governance',prompt:'A prompt alters tool behavior but skips review because weights are fixed. What is missing?',answer:0,options:[['Behavior-based change control for the full release.','Prompts can alter risk.'],['Only more training rows.','The gate is the problem.'],['An invented passing score.','Not supplied or relevant.'],['Removal of monitoring.','Monitoring remains needed.']]},
{concept:'Documentation lineage',prompt:'A card describes v4 while production serves v9. What is weakened?',answer:1,options:[['Only length.','The evidence is mismatched.'],['Auditability and relevance of limitations/evaluation.','Documentation must match deployment.'],['Only GPU use.','Unrelated.'],['Nothing if names match.','Versions change behavior.']]},
{concept:'Risk classification',prompt:'Should drafting and autonomous triage use identical gates?',answer:2,options:[['Yes, all AI consequences are equal.','They differ greatly.'],['Yes, size is the only risk factor.','Use context matters.'],['No; controls follow use, harm and reversibility.','Risk is application-specific.'],['Neither needs governance.','Both need proportionate control.']]},
{concept:'Ethical escalation',prompt:'Material subgroup harm is found before release but root cause is incomplete. What should happen?',answer:3,options:[['Stay silent until certainty.','Harm may proceed.'],['Delete evidence.','That defeats accountability.'],['Approve privately.','Risk acceptance belongs to accountable roles.'],['Document uncertainty and use pause/escalation.','Incomplete diagnosis need not block responsible action.']]},
{concept:'Agent privilege',prompt:'A read-only research agent has unrestricted email and database writes. What is best?',answer:0,options:[['Remove unnecessary capabilities and scope remaining tools.','Least privilege limits mistaken effects.'],['Add more writes.','That expands risk.'],['Use only polite instructions.','They are not enforceable permission boundaries.'],['Hide tool names.','Capabilities remain.']]},
{concept:'Fairness trade-off',prompt:'Two fairness criteria conflict under different base rates. What is professional practice?',answer:1,options:[['Claim both are satisfied anyway.','That is false.'],['Explain conflict, harms and chosen criterion with accountable stakeholders.','The trade-off needs judgment.'],['Stop measuring groups.','That hides risk.'],['Use accuracy forever.','It does not resolve the conflict.']]}
]});
})();
